 【45†3.8.6. RISC-V】\nL46:       7. 【46†3.8.7. Jolt 0.1.0】\nL47:       8. 【47†3.8.8. Additional resources】\nL48:   6. 【48†4. Roadmap】\nL49:   7.     1. 【49†4.1. Streaming】\nL50:     2. 【50†4.2. Recursion】\nL51:     3. 【51†4.3. On-chain verifier】\nL52:   8. 【52†5. Testing gates】\nL53: # JoltBook\nL54: # 【53†EQ polynomial optimizations】\nL55: \nL56: Many sumcheck instances in Jolt have summands of the form\nL57: \nL58: g(x 1​,…,x n​)=eq​(w,x)⋅p(x)\nL59: \nL60: where w∈F n is a verifier challenge vector, eq​ is the 【54†equality polynomial】, and p(x) is a product of witness polynomials. In round i, the prover must compute the round polynomial s i​(X)=x′∈0,1 n−i∑​eq​(w,r 1​,…,r i−1​,X,x′)⋅p(r 1​,…,r i−1​,X,x′), for X evaluated at a small set of points, and send those evaluations.\nL61: A naive implementation materializes eq​(w,⋅) as a dense table of 2 n field elements and 【55†binds】 it alongside witness tables each round.\nL62: \nL63: Jolt uses two prover-side optimizations that (a) avoid binding a length-2 n eq table each round and (b) avoid ever materializing a full 2 n-entry eq table at all, while keeping the standard sumcheck transcript/verifier unchanged.\nL64: ## 【56†Dao–Thaler: factor linearization + split-eq tables】\nL65: \nL66: The 【57†[Dao, Thaler, 2024]†eprint.iacr.org】 paper describes two (related) optimizations to sumchecks involving the eq​ polynomial that are widely applicable throughout Jolt.\nL67: ### 【58†Current-factor linearization】\nL68: \nL69: The equality polynomial factors into univariate terms: eq​(w,x)=j=1∏n​(w j​x j​+(1−w j​)(1−x j​)).\nL70: \nL71: After binding k variables to challenges r 1​,…,r k​, the restricted eq polynomial is eq​(w,(r 1​,…,r k​,x k+1​,…,x n​))=s (accumulated scalar)j=1∏k​(w j​r j​+(1−w j​)(1−r j​))​​⋅j=k+1∏n​(w j​x j​+(1−w j​)(1−x j​)).\nL72: \nL73: In round i=k+1, the eq contribution for the current variable is the known linear function ℓ i​(X)=s⋅((1−w i​)(1−X)+w i​X).\nL74: So we can write s i​(X)=ℓ i​(X)⋅q i​(X), where q i​(X)=x′∈0,1 n−i∑​E rest​(x′)⋅p(r 1​,…,r i−1​,X,x′),E rest​(x′)=j>i∏​(w j​x j​+(1−w j​)(1−x j​)).\nL75: \nL76: Rather than \"binding\" a full 2 n eq table each round, we maintain the scalar s (updated in O(1) per round) and treat the current eq factor as a known linear multiplier ℓ i​(X). This removes the O(2 n) per-round cost of binding a dense eq table.\nL77: ### 【59†Split-eq tables】\nL78: \nL79: The remaining factor E rest​(x′) still ranges over the (n−i) unbound variables. Materializing it as a dense table would reintroduce 2 n-scale memory. Dao–Thaler avoid this with a two-layer decomposition: split the remaining variables into two halves and rewrite the sum as an iterated sum.\nL80: \nL81: Concretely, choose a split parameter m≈(n−i)/2. Write the remaining suffix variables as x′=(x out​,x in​),w′=(w out​,w in​), so E rest​(x′)=eq​(w out​,x out​)⋅eq​(w in​,x in​).\nL82: Instead of a single 2 n−i-entry table, we keep two smaller tables whose total size is O(2(n−i)/2):\nL83: \nL84:   * E out​[x out​]=eq​(w out​,x out​)\nL85:   * E in​[x in​]=eq​(w in​,x in​)\nL86: \nL87: Then the inner polynomial evaluation becomes q i​(X)=x out​∑​E out​[x out​]⋅(x in​∑​E in​[x in​]⋅p(r 1​,…,r i−1​,X,x out​,x in​)).\nL88: \nL89: This structure:\nL90:   * reduces prover memory by a square-root factor (two tables of size O(2 n/2) instead of one of size O(2 n))\nL91:   * reduces the number of field multiplications performed by the prover by a square root factor (same reason)\nL92: ## 【60†Gruen's optimization: recover one evaluation from the previous round's claim】\nL93: \nL94: Even after the above, a degree-d round polynomial typically requires d+1 evaluations s i​(X) at chosen points. Jolt uses an optimization introduced in Section 3.2 of 【61†[Gruen, 2024]†eprint.iacr.org】: recover one needed evaluation from the round-sum constraint, so the expensive inner-sum machinery only runs for the remaining points.\nL95: Note that this optimization (as implemented in Jolt) does not change what is sent to the verifier.\nL96: ### 【62†Degree-3 case】\nL97: \nL98: Assume the inner polynomial has degree 2: q i​(X)=e X 2+d X+c.\nL99: \nL100: In Jolt we compute enough to get:\nL101: \nL102:   * q i​(0)=c (constant term), and\nL103:   * the leading coefficient e, and we avoid directly computing q i​(1) via another full inner sum.\nL104: Because the sumcheck relation gives the verifier-checked identity s i​(0)+s i​(1)=claim i​, and s i​(X)=ℓ i​(X)q i​(X), we can solve for the missing evaluation: q i​(1)=ℓ i​(1)claim i​−ℓ i​(0)q i​(0)​. When ℓ i​(1)=0 (or ℓ i​(0)=0), the code skips the division and evaluates the missing endpoint q i​(1) (or q i​(0)) directly from the retained tables. When the accumulated eq prefix is zero, s i​ is the zero polynomial and the code emits it without dividing.\nL105: Every challenge is handled, so completeness holds over the full 128-bit challenge space rather than only with overwhelming probability.\nL106: Once q i​(0), q i​(1), and the leading coefficient e are known, we can evaluate q i​(2) and q i​(3) cheaply (no additional inner-hypercube sums), and then compute s i​(t)=ℓ i​(t)q i​(t)for t∈{0,1,2,3}, and interpolate the cubic s i​(X) from its four evaluations.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06022v1\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06022v1: (400) Content length is too large: 5522678--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://upcommons.upc.edu/entities/publication/7d5f3945-e5ed-4354-abda-5b509ea8a854\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://upcommons.upc.edu/entities/publication/7d5f3945-e5ed-4354-abda-5b509ea8a854: (403) Forbidden"